  "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!\u226b{e}^{n}\u226b{n}^{m}\u226b\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $\u226b$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}\u2061(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
